course: dictionary
lesson: 6995
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one the writer means. The word «parade» can mean a big procession of bands and floats through the streets, or it can mean showing something off proudly. Read the words around it to decide.
explain: «The biggest is France's national day: a parade in Paris in the morning, fireworks at night and dances at the fire station.» Fireworks and dances are clues that a parade is a celebration, with people moving through the streets together.
explain: «When Otter tries to parade him round the room, Toad says gently that Badger was the mastermind.» Toad is modest and does not want the attention. So parade here means showing someone off so people will notice. Check it by swapping in your meaning: "tries to show him off round the room" still makes sense.
fact: A parade is a procession of people, bands or floats moving through the streets to celebrate something.
fact: A parade can also be a line of soldiers standing or marching for inspection.
fact: To parade means to walk or march in a parade, or to show something off proudly so people will notice.
fact: In the UK, a parade can be a row of shops, as in «a shopping parade».
fact: Parades happen on holidays such as St Patrick's Day, Thanksgiving, Anzac Day and Christmas.
fact: At a big parade, stay with your adult and agree a meeting point in case you get separated.
exercise: «The biggest is France's national day: a parade in Paris in the morning, fireworks at night and dances at the fire station.» Which meaning of parade fits best? | *A procession of people moving through the streets to celebrate | To show something off so people notice | A line of soldiers in a fort | A row of shops in a street
exercise: «When Otter tries to parade him round the room, Toad says gently that Badger was the mastermind.» What does parade mean here? | To fall asleep in a chair | *To show someone off proudly so people will notice | To march in a line with a brass band | To buy things in a shop
exercise: Which phrase uses parade to mean a row of shops? | *A shopping parade | A street parade | A passing-out parade | A May Day parade
test: Ella says, «We paraded our new kites in the park.» What does paraded mean? | *Showed them off proudly so people noticed | Flew the kites very high | Sold the kites in a row of shops | Marched in a line for inspection
test: Before a big parade, what should you agree with your adult? | How many sweets to bring | Which float you will ride on | The name of the band | *Where to meet if you get separated
exam: How can you check which meaning of a word fits a sentence? | Skip the sentence and never come back to it | Look only at the first word of the sentence | Choose the longest meaning | *Swap your meaning into the sentence and see if it still makes sense
